Facility Risk Assessments—to evaluate pipeline companies’ compliance with safety regulations and identify vulnerabilities. These assessments use a scorecard system called the Corporate Security Review Score (CSR). While this approach provides some information about pipeline vulnerability, it lacks transparency because the CSR scores are not publicly disclosed.

The report also highlights several shortfalls in TSA’s pipeline security program:

*   Lack of clarity in the definition of “critical facility”;
*   Inadequate workforce planning and training;
*   Limited resources allocated to pipeline security activities;
*   Insufficient data quality control measures;
*   Failure to update the risk assessment methodology since 2004.
*   A lack of peer-reviewed metrics to measure progress toward improving pipeline security;

GAO recommends that TSA take steps to improve these weaknesses, which could help prevent potential attacks on the country’s pipeline network. Some recommendations include:

*   Developing a strategic plan to guide pipeline security efforts
*   Enhancing workforce development programs
*   Increasing funding for pipeline security initiatives
*   Implementing more robust performance measurement tools
*   Conducting an independent evaluation of the pipeline security program
*   Updating the risk assessment methodology to better account for evolving threats and interdependencies between sectors.

These actions would enable TSA to provide effective support to pipeline owners and operators, ultimately enhancing national security.
